		<a href="#section-security-routes" id="section-security-routes" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Which security route handles what?</h2>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				These mechanisms solve different problems. In a normal production setup you will usually use verified TLS for submission, then add DKIM and
				one content-protection format only when your mail flow calls for them. SPF and DMARC are included here for context, but they are not configured
				by Simple Java Mail.
			</p>
			<div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable email security route comparison">
				<table class="security-route-table">
					<thead>
						<tr><th>Route</th><th>What it protects or proves</th><th>Who checks it</th><th>DMARC role</th><th>Where it lives</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><th>TLS / STARTTLS</th><td>The SMTP hop between your application and its submission server</td><td>Your application and SMTP server</td><td>None</td><td>Simple Java Mail core</td></tr>
						<tr><th>REQUIRETLS</th><td>Whether supporting SMTP relays must use authenticated TLS for onward delivery</td><td>Your submission server and later supporting relays</td><td>None</td><td>Simple Java Mail core; RFC 8689 server support required</td></tr>
						<tr><th>OAuth2</th><td>Your application's right to use an SMTP account, without giving the mailer a mailbox password</td><td>The SMTP server</td><td>None</td><td>Simple Java Mail core plus your token provider</td></tr>
						<tr><th>DKIM</th><td>A signing domain and the integrity of the signed headers and body</td><td>Receiving mail systems</td><td>Can satisfy DMARC when the valid DKIM signing domain aligns with the visible From domain</td><td><code>dkim-module</code> plus the domain's DNS record</td></tr>
						<tr><th>SPF</th><td>Whether the sending server is authorized for the SMTP envelope domain</td><td>Receiving mail systems</td><td>Can satisfy DMARC when SPF passes and the envelope domain aligns with the visible From domain</td><td>Sending infrastructure and DNS, outside this library</td></tr>
						<tr><th>DMARC</th><td>How aligned DKIM or SPF results should be evaluated for the visible From domain</td><td>Receiving mail systems</td><td>This is the policy and evaluation itself</td><td>Domain DNS and the receiver, outside this library</td></tr>
						<tr><th>S/MIME</th><td>Message-content signatures and encryption using certificates</td><td>The recipient's client or your receiving code</td><td>None</td><td><code>smime-module</code></td></tr>
						<tr><th>OpenPGP/MIME</th><td>Message-content signatures and encryption using OpenPGP keys</td><td>The recipient's client or your receiving code</td><td>None</td><td><code>openpgp-module</code></td></tr>
						<tr><th>CRLF scanning</th><td>Your generated headers against newline injection from untrusted values</td><td>Simple Java Mail before sending</td><td>None</td><td>Simple Java Mail core</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="wide">
				DMARC checks alignment with the domain in the visible <code class="inline language-text">From</code> header. For the DKIM route, configure
				<code class="inline language-java">dkimSigningDomain(...)</code> for an aligned domain. SPF uses the SMTP
				<code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> identity and the sending server's DNS authorization. A Simple Java Mail bounce address can
				set that envelope identity, but it cannot publish SPF or make the sending IP pass. See
				<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9989.html">RFC 9989</a> for the current DMARC model.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				S/MIME and OpenPGP/MIME can authenticate and protect content under your application's trust rules, but receivers do not use either one for
				DMARC. TLS and OAuth2 protect the submission path and account access, not the From domain.
			</p>
		</section>

