		<a href="#section-authentication-methods" id="section-authentication-methods" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Choosing SMTP encryption and authentication</h2>

		<section>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					The transport strategy chooses how your Mailer connects. Unless you configure another strategy, the default is
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code>: try STARTTLS when the server offers it and allow plaintext when it does not.
					Adding a username and password does not change that choice. If your application requires encryption, choose
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> or <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code> explicitly.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					There are four strategies:
				</p>
				<pre><code>TransportStrategy strategy = TransportStrategy.SMTP;
// or: TransportStrategy.SMTPS, TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS,
//     TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2, TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2

SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
  .withSMTPServer("host", port, "username", passwordOrOAUTH2Token)
  .withTransportStrategy(strategy);
</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					Or with property default:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTP
# or: SMTPS, SMTP_TLS, SMTP_OAUTH2, SMTPS_OAUTH2
</code></pre>
				<div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable SMTP transport strategy comparison">
					<table class="security-route-table">
						<thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>TLS behavior</th><th>When STARTTLS is absent</th><th>Default port</th></tr></thead>
						<tbody>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP</code> (default)</th><td>Try STARTTLS when offered</td><td>May continue in plaintext, including password authentication</td><td>25</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP_TLS</code></th><td>Require STARTTLS before authentication or mail</td><td>Fail the connection</td><td>587</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTPS</code></th><td>TLS from the start of the connection</td><td>Not applicable; no STARTTLS upgrade</td><td>465</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTP_OAUTH2</code></th><td>Require STARTTLS before sending the access token</td><td>Fail the connection</td><td>587</td></tr>
							<tr><th><code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code></th><td>TLS from the start, then XOAUTH2 authentication</td><td>Not applicable; no STARTTLS upgrade</td><td>465</td></tr>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<p class="wide">
					<code class="inline language-java">buildMailer()</code> rejects an extra
					<code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.starttls.required</code> value that disables STARTTLS when you select
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> or <code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code>.
					Remove that override to keep mandatory TLS. For opportunistic username/password authentication, choose
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code> instead. See the <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#mandatory-starttls">migration example</a>.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					For <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code>, an extra <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> must retain <code>smtps</code>;
					a contradictory protocol override is rejected before connections or workers are started.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					This check runs when Simple Java Mail creates the Session, including logging-only builds. A caller-supplied Session or
					<code class="inline language-java">CustomMailer</code> keeps its own connection configuration. The check does not freeze the Session,
					validate arbitrary provider or socket-factory replacements, or change your certificate-trust and server-identity settings.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					A <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a> can help explain what happened on its own connection;
					it does not choose a strategy or approve a later send. Configure the required behavior on the Mailer that sends the email.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtp" id="section-transport-strategy-smtp" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP</h3>

		<section>
			<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP);</code></pre>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					This is the default strategy for both unauthenticated relays and username/password connections. With opportunistic TLS enabled,
					it attempts a TLS upgrade when the server advertises STARTTLS. If the server does not advertise it, the connection can continue
					in plaintext. Password authentication is not protected in that case; base64 encoding is not encryption.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					Whenever TLS is negotiated, Simple Java Mail verifies that the certificate belongs to the SMTP host and uses the JVM trust store.
					Both checks can be overridden, but that
					should be reserved for controlled compatibility or test environments.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					Fallback means STARTTLS was not offered. If the server offers it but refuses the upgrade, or the TLS handshake or certificate check
					fails, the connection fails. Simple Java Mail does not automatically retry that connection in plaintext.
				</p>
				<p class="wide">
					This strategy can still fall back to plaintext when the server does not advertise STARTTLS. An active attacker can therefore
					suppress the STARTTLS offer before certificate validation begins. Use
					<code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTPS</code> or
					<code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS</code> when encryption must be mandatory.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Plain SMTP attempts an optional STARTTLS upgrade by default. For a server that breaks during STARTTLS negotiation, you can turn that attempt off
					programmatically or with
					<code class="inline language-properties">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls</code>.
					This escape hatch applies only to <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy.SMTP</code>. It does not change mandatory TLS,
					OAuth2 transport, or SMTPS.
					Turning it off deliberately skips STARTTLS even when offered; it is not a fallback after a failed handshake.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
      <pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	  .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP)
	  .withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	      .withOpportunisticTLS(false)
	      .build());
</code></pre>

Or with properties:
<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.opportunistic.tls=false
</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtps" id="section-transport-strategy-smtps" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTPS</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				SMTP entirely encapsulated by TLS. Commonly known as SMTPS.
			</p>
			<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS);</code></pre>

			<p class="wide">
				Strict certificate validation is the default. Unless you configure an explicit exception, server certificates must be issued
			</p>
			<ol class="indent">
				<li>by a certificate authority in the JVM trust store; and</li>
				<li>to a subject matching the identity of the remote SMTP server.</li>
			</ol>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-tls" id="section-transport-strategy-tls" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				The connection starts with SMTP and must upgrade to TLS before authentication or mail submission. A username and password work here too;
				there is no fallback to plaintext if the server lacks STARTTLS.
			</p>
			<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "username", "password")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS);</code></pre>

			<p class="wide">
				Strict certificate validation is the default. Unless you configure an explicit exception, server certificates must be issued
			</p>
			<ol class="indent">
				<li>by a certificate authority in the JVM trust store; and</li>
				<li>to a subject matching the identity of the remote SMTP server.</li>
			</ol>
		</section>
		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-oauth2" id="section-transport-strategy-oauth2" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2</h3>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_OAUTH2</code> uses normal SMTP submission but authenticates with the
				<code class="inline language-text">XOAUTH2</code> mechanism instead of a mailbox password. It uses port 587 by default and requires STARTTLS,
				so the connection is encrypted before authentication.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				The SMTP username identifies the mailbox and the credential is an OAuth2 access token, not a refresh token, client secret, or authorization
				code. If your application already has a token whose lifetime covers the Mailer, pass it in the usual password position.
			</p>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user@example.com", accessToken)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail configures SMTP and XOAUTH2, but it does not run the OAuth authorization flow itself. Your application remains responsible
				for obtaining the access token from its provider.
			</p>
		</section>
		<a href="#section-transport-strategy-smtps-oauth2" id="section-transport-strategy-smtps-oauth2" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2</h3>
		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				Use <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code> when the SMTP service requires XOAUTH2 over implicit TLS. The connection is encrypted from its
				first byte, uses port 465 when no port is supplied, and does not negotiate STARTTLS. Certificate-chain and server-identity
				validation follow the same defaults as <code>SMTPS</code>. Both OAuth2 strategies accept a fixed token or a runtime provider.
			</p>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 465, "user@example.com", accessToken)
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">The existing property configuration also accepts the preset:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.transportstrategy=SMTPS_OAUTH2
simplejavamail.smtp.host=smtp.example.com
simplejavamail.smtp.username=user@example.com
# Fixed-token route only; omit this property when registering a provider in Java.
simplejavamail.smtp.password=your-access-token</code></pre>
		</section>
		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				For a long-lived Mailer, use an <code class="inline language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider</code> so an expired token can be replaced without
				rebuilding the Mailer. It works with both OAuth2 strategies and can use whichever OAuth client, token endpoint, cache, or refresh flow your application already uses.
			</p>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">OAuth2AccessTokenProvider accessTokens =
    () -&gt; tokenService.currentAccessToken();

Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587, "user@example.com")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_OAUTH2)
    .withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(accessTokens)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">For the same provider over implicit TLS, choose port 465 and <code>SMTPS_OAUTH2</code>:</p>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Mailer mailer = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    .withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 465, "user@example.com")
    .withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTPS_OAUTH2)
    .withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(accessTokens)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail asks the provider immediately before opening a physical SMTP connection and again when a disconnected connection must be
				reopened. An already-connected pooled transport is reused without another provider call. The provider should be thread-safe and cache a valid
				token rather than contacting the authorization server on every call.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				Choose either a fixed token or a provider for one Mailer; configuring both is rejected. Provider errors and null or blank results stop the
				connection without exposing the token, while SMTP authentication rejection is returned normally without a hidden retry.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				A supplied Jakarta Mail Session can use the provider too;
				configure that Session for <code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtp.auth.mechanisms=XOAUTH2</code>, or
				<code class="inline language-properties">mail.smtps.auth.mechanisms=XOAUTH2</code> when its protocol is <code>smtps</code>, and then call
				<code class="inline language-java">mail.mailerBuilder(session).withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(...)</code>.
			</p>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-mechanisms">
				Set <code>mail.transport.protocol</code> on the Session or Mailer builder to select the intended connection protocol. A token provider
				requires <code>XOAUTH2</code> in that protocol's <code>auth.mechanisms</code> property. If you reuse a Session, wait for the previous
				Mailer to finish shutting down before changing its credentials and building another Mailer. See the
				<a href="understanding-configuration.html#supplied-session-protocol">protocol settings and conflict rules</a>.
			</p>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">// The previous Mailer using this Session has completed close().
try (Mailer reused = mail.mailerBuilder(session)
        .withProperty("mail.transport.protocol", "smtp")
        .withProperty("mail.smtp.auth.mechanisms", "XOAUTH2") // for an smtp Session
        .withOAuth2AccessTokenProvider(accessTokens)
        .buildMailer()) {
    reused.sync().sendMail(email);
}</code></pre>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-usernames">
				For SMTP or STARTTLS, configure <code>mail.smtp.user</code>; for SMTPS, configure <code>mail.smtps.user</code>.
				This works with direct and pooled connections. A different username configured for the other protocol does not change the account used.
				Jakarta Mail also supports a username in the transport URL and the <code>mail.user</code> fallback.
				See <a href="/migration-notes-10.0.0.html#pooled-oauth-usernames">pooled OAuth username migration guidance</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-registration">
				After a Mailer has fully shut down, you can reuse its Session with a fixed token, a different token provider or ordinary authentication.
				Configure the credentials you want for the next Mailer, along with the matching authentication mechanism and TLS settings.
				Do not change the Session while its Mailer is still in use. See
				<a href="/migration-notes-10.0.0.html#pooled-oauth-registration">pooled authentication migration guidance</a> for a fixed-token example.
			</p>
			<p class="wide" id="supplied-session-oauth2-token">
				For a Session you supply, set a fixed token with the String property <code>simplejavamail.oauth2.token</code>.
				String values inherited through <code>Properties</code> defaults are accepted too. Configure the Session's protocol, username and
				XOAUTH2 mechanism as usual. Sending, connection tests and authenticated probes all use that token.
				See the <a href="/understanding-configuration.html#supplied-sessions">supplied-Session rules</a> and
				<a href="/migration-notes-10.0.0.html#inherited-oauth2-token">inherited-token migration note</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				Spring applications can register the provider as a bean. See <a href="/spring.html#section-spring-oauth2">Spring integration</a> for the direct
				bean form and an adapter for Spring Security's OAuth2 client manager.
			</p>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-requiretls" id="section-requiretls" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Require TLS for onward delivery</h2>

		<section>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					A Mailer's <code class="inline language-java">TransportStrategy</code> controls the connection from your application to its configured SMTP
					server. It says nothing about the next hop. For an individual message that must not be relayed over plaintext, request RFC 8689 REQUIRETLS on
					the Email:
				</p>
			</div>
			<pre class="full-width-example"><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .from("sender@example.org")
    .withRecipients(mail.recipientBuilder().withName("Recipient").withAddress("recipient@example.net").withType(Message.RecipientType.TO).build())
    .withSubject("Sensitive report")
    .withPlainText("...")
    .withSmtpDeliveryOptions(SmtpDeliveryOptions.builder()
        .withTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery()
        .build())
    .buildEmail();

MailSubmissionReceipt receipt = mailer.sync().sendMail(email);
if (receipt.isRequireTlsUsed()) {
    audit.record("REQUIRETLS was issued for this SMTP attempt");
}</code></pre>
			<div>
				<p class="wide">
					The bundled Angus adapter checks the actual send connection immediately before submission. It requires TLS, server-identity checking, a trusted
					certificate policy, and a post-TLS REQUIRETLS advertisement. If one is missing, no <code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code> command is
					sent. For example:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-text small">This email requires REQUIRETLS, but the SMTP server does not advertise usable REQUIRETLS support after TLS negotiation.
Use a server that supports RFC 8689, or clear the requirement for this email. No message was submitted.</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					This check is intentionally separate from a <a href="debugging.html#section-smtp-capabilities">connection probe</a>, which uses another connection.
					<code class="inline language-java">SMTP_TLS</code> and <code class="inline language-java">SMTPS</code> do not enable REQUIRETLS automatically.
					The default opportunistic <code class="inline language-java">SMTP</code> strategy can satisfy it when STARTTLS actually succeeds and all checks pass.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					To apply the same suppressible default through property files, environment variables or Spring configuration, set:
				</p>
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.requiretls=true</code></pre>
				<p>
					Calling <code class="inline language-java">clearTlsRequiredForOnwardDelivery()</code> removes a value set directly on that builder and resumes the
					normal defaults/overrides fallback. To opt one composed Email out of a configured default or override, suppress that source explicitly.
					Exact EML accepts the same <code class="inline language-java">SmtpDeliveryOptions</code> requirement, but keeps bypassing Email templates and preserves
					its source bytes unchanged.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
				<pre><code class="language-java">Email email = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .withTemplateOptions(EmailTemplateOptions.builder()
        .dontApplyDefaultValueFor(
            EmailProperty.TLS_REQUIRED_FOR_ONWARD_DELIVERY)
        .dontApplyOverrideValueFor(
            EmailProperty.TLS_REQUIRED_FOR_ONWARD_DELIVERY)
        .build())
    // compose the email
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				A true <code class="inline language-java">isRequireTlsUsed()</code> means the first server received REQUIRETLS on this attempt, including a rejected
				<code class="inline language-text">MAIL FROM</code>. It does not prove server acceptance, compliance by every later relay, final delivery, or
				end-to-end confidentiality. Use <a href="#section-sending-smime">S/MIME</a> or
				<a href="#section-sending-openpgp">OpenPGP/MIME</a> when the message content itself needs cryptographic protection.
			</p>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-custom-ssl-factory" id="section-custom-ssl-factory" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Configure your own SSL connection factory</h2>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Supply your own SSL connection factory when you need custom connection code, for example to use a client certificate or your own certificate checks.
				</p>
				<p>With an Angus Session created by Simple Java Mail, a failure in your factory stops the connection attempt. It does not quietly try
					Java's standard factory instead: that factory might not use your client certificate or custom certificate checks.
					You can explicitly allow that fallback, as shown below, if the standard factory is also acceptable for your deployment.</p>
				<p>If your factory fails while adding encryption to an already-open connection, Simple Java Mail closes that connection so it is not left hanging.
					Your original factory still controls certificate validation and any trusted-server checks. The cleanup does not add cancellation support
					for a custom factory. Sessions you create and supply yourself keep their existing behavior.</p>
				<p>A class-based factory needs a public static <code>getDefault()</code> method. In a modular application, export its package to
					<code>org.simplejavamail.mailprovider.angus</code>, or supply an instance instead. Factory initialization stays lazy.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .withCustomSSLFactoryClass(theClassName) // or:
	    .withCustomSSLFactoryInstance(theInstance) // takes precedence
	    .build())
	.buildMailer();
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.custom.sslfactory.class=your.project.YourSSLSocketFactory</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>
		<section>
			<div class="wide">
				<h3>Explicitly allow the standard factory as a fallback</h3>
				<p>This uses an existing advanced Angus property, not another builder method. Set it only when a connection made without your custom factory's
					settings is acceptable. For <code>SMTP</code> and <code>SMTP_TLS</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java">mailerBuilder.withProperty("mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback", "true");</code></pre>
				<p>Or in your Simple Java Mail properties file or Spring configuration:</p>
<pre><code class="language-properties">simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtp.socketFactory.fallback=true</code></pre>
				<p>For <code>SMTPS</code>, use <code>mail.smtps.socketFactory.fallback</code> in Java or
					<code>simplejavamail.extraproperties.mail.smtps.socketFactory.fallback</code> in external configuration.
					An explicit <code>false</code> keeps fallback disabled. This setting concerns your custom factory failing;
					it does not allow retrying in plaintext after a failed STARTTLS upgrade.</p>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-whitelist-hosts" id="section-whitelist-hosts" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Certificate trust</h2>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Simple Java Mail leaves <code class="inline language-properties">mail.*.ssl.trust</code> unset by default.
					Angus Mail then validates the certificate chain against the JVM trust store.
				</p>
				<p>
					Certificate trust and server identity are two separate checks. A normal production connection needs both: a certificate from a trusted issuer
					and a certificate that names the SMTP host you connected to.
				</p>
				<p>
					For a private certificate authority, the best fix is to add that CA to the JVM trust store. If that is not practical,
					<code class="inline language-java">trustingSSLHosts(...)</code> creates a narrow exception for named hosts.
					<code class="inline language-java">trustingAllHosts(true)</code> accepts certificates from every host and should be a last-resort compatibility setting.
				</p>
				<p>In the unreleased 10.0.0 line, different explicit Java, raw-property, factory and supplied Session declarations for the same
          supported TLS setting fail during construction by default. Remove obsolete trust workarounds at their sources, or deliberately select
          the advanced <a href="understanding-configuration.html#connection-conflicts">override policy</a>. Locks and mandatory encryption requirements remain
          binding in either mode.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
				<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587)
	.withTransportStrategy(TransportStrategy.SMTP_TLS)
	// secure default; shown explicitly here
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingAllHosts(false)
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(true)
	    .build());

// narrow exception when the issuing CA cannot be added to the JVM trust store
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingSSLHosts("smtp.internal.example")
	    .build());

// broad compatibility escape hatch; avoid in production
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .trustingAllHosts(true)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.trustallhosts=false
# optional narrow exception; ignored when trustallhosts is true:
simplejavamail.defaults.trustedhosts=smtp.internal.example</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-verify-server-identity" id="section-verify-server-identity" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Verifying server identity</h2>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail enables server identity verification for TLS connections by default (also see <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2595.txt">RFC 2595</a>,
				2.4. Server Identity Check). Angus Mail checks that the certificate names the host used to start the connection.
			</p>
			<p class="wide">
				This does not replace certificate-chain validation. Keep both checks enabled for normal production use, including when a private CA has been added
				to the JVM trust store. Disabling hostname verification is intended only for controlled compatibility or testing scenarios.
			</p>
<pre><code>// enabled by default
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.example.com", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(true)
	    .build());

// compatibility escape hatch; avoid in production
SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder().withSMTPServer("smtp.internal.example", 587)
	.withTlsConfig(TlsConfig.builder()
	    .verifyingServerIdentity(false)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
				Or with property default:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small">simplejavamail.defaults.verifyserveridentity=true</code></pre>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-crlf-scanning" id="section-crlf-scanning" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		<h2>Scanning for suspicious content</h2>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				Simple Java Mail by default tests most fields and headers for suspicious content, which could indicate a
				<strong>CRLF injection attack</strong>. This is a unique feature of Simple Java Mail.
			</p>

			<p class="wide" style="margin-top: 1em">
				The values being scanned are:
			</p>
			<ul class="indent">
				<li>subject</li>
				<li>additional outgoing header names and values, including explicit choices</li>
				<li>every attachment name, content ID, nested datasource name and description</li>
				<li>every embedded image name, content ID, nested datasource name and description</li>
				<li>from recipient name and address</li>
				<li>every replyTo recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>bounceTo recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>every TO/CC/BCC recipient name and address</li>
				<li>disposition-notification-to recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li>return-receipt-to recipient name and address, if provided</li>
				<li><a href="/features.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">fixed DSN envelope identifiers (ENVID)</a>, validated separately when their configuration is built</li>
			</ul>

			<p class="wide">
				Fixed envelope identifiers accept only printable ASCII, so CR, LF, tabs and other control characters are rejected before an Email can be built.
				The bundled Angus adapter encodes spaces, <code>+</code> and <code>=</code> just before sending, so an identifier cannot add another SMTP parameter.
				For example, <code>order RET=FULL</code> becomes <code>ENVID=order+20RET+3DFULL</code>, not a separate <code>RET</code> setting.
				Raw MAIL extensions and custom transport implementations remain caller-owned.
			</p>

			<p class="wide">
				Here's some more info on this topic:
			</p>
			<ul class="indent">
				<li><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20160331233647/http://www.cakesolutions.net/teamblogs/2008/05/08/email-header-injection-security">Email Header Injection security</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://security.stackexchange.com/questions/54099/what-threats-come-from-crlf-in-email-generation/54100#54100">StackExchange - What threats come from CRLF in email generation?</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://archive.ph/NuETu">OWASP - Testing for IMAP SMTP Injection</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://archive.ph/uReuD">CWE-93: Improper Neutralization of CRLF Sequences ('CRLF Injection')</a></li>
			</ul>

			<p class="wide">
				Use the following setting to log ordinary client-validation findings, including header-scan findings, as warnings instead of blocking the send.
				The checks still run. ENVID validation and encoding are unaffected: an invalid fixed envelope identifier is still rejected.
			</p>
<pre><code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
	.withValidationConfig(ValidationConfig.builder()
	    .withClientValidationWarningsOnly(true)
	    .build());
</code></pre>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-sending-dkim" id="section-sending-dkim" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 dkim=true}}Signing emails with DKIM{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Simple Java Mail supports signing with <a href="https://www.endpointdev.com/blog/2014/04/spf-dkim-and-dmarc-brief-explanation/">DKIM</a>
					domain keys on individual emails or reusable Email defaults/overrides templates.
				</p>
                <p>The sender domain must match the signing domain or be one of its subdomains. This comparison ignores capitalization:
                    <code>Sender@EXAMPLE.COM</code> can use <code>example.com</code>, as can <code>Sender@sales.Example.Com</code>.
                    The address, its local part and your configured signing domain retain their supplied spelling. The usual DNS key
                    and signing requirements still apply.</p>
				<p>
					Use <code class="inline language-java">dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path)</code>,
					<code class="inline language-java">dkimPrivateKeyPath(String)</code>, or
					<code class="inline language-java">dkimPrivateKeyPath(File)</code> for a key file; it is read immediately and
					the stream Simple Java Mail opens is closed before the call returns.
					For key material already in memory, <code class="inline language-java">dkimPrivateKeyData(byte[])</code>
					copies the bytes. The <code class="inline language-java">String</code> overload converts the supplied key data to
					UTF-8 bytes without Base64-decoding it. The <code class="inline language-java">InputStream</code> overload consumes
					the stream immediately but leaves closing it to the caller.
				</p>
				<p>Complete key-material reads for DKIM, S/MIME and PKCS12 report known opening, reading and closing I/O failures as
                    <code>MailException</code> with kind <code>PREPARATION</code>. The original I/O cause and suppressed closing errors remain available.
                    Filesystem readers close their own streams; supplied streams remain open for your code to close. This does not perform
                    cryptographic validation before the feature is used. A filesystem replacement is installed only after reading,
                    preparation and closing all succeed. If the call fails, a reused builder keeps its previous material and related choices.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="small">currentEmailBuilder.signWithDomainKey(
	DkimConfig.builder()
		.dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path.of("secrets/dkim-private-key.der"))
		.dkimSigningDomain("your-domain.org")
		.dkimSelector("dkim1")
		.useLengthParam(false) // default
		.build()
);
</code></pre>
				Or configure DKIM as a default for emails sent through a mailer:
				<pre><code class="small">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults();
Email signingDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithDomainKey(DkimConfig.builder()
            .dkimPrivateKeyData(privateKeyBytes)
            .dkimSigningDomain("your_domain.org")
            .dkimSelector("your_selector")
            .build())
    .buildEmail();

currentMailerBuilder
    .withEmailDefaults(signingDefaults)
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
				<p>
					The template can omit the sender; the prepared email cannot. A message's own DKIM setting wins over a default.
					A supplied template replaces property-derived message defaults, so see
					<a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer">defaults and overrides</a> if you want to keep those as well.
				</p>
				Or with properties:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small"># defaults on Mailer level:
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.private_key_file_or_data=file:my_dkim_key.der
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.selector=dkim1
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.signing_domain=your-domain.com
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.use_length_param=false # default
				# Omit header exclusions to sign the default header set, including From.
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.header_canonicalization=RELAXED
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.body_canonicalization=RELAXED
				simplejavamail.dkim.signing.algorithm=SHA256_WITH_RSA
				</code></pre>
				<p class="wide">
					Use <code class="inline language-properties">file:</code> for a path or
					<code class="inline language-properties">base64:</code> followed by Base64-encoded key bytes for inline data.
					A missing explicit file or malformed Base64 value fails during configuration without including the key data in the error.
					Unprefixed values are also accepted for compatibility.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p><strong>Use the exceptions deliberately</strong></p>
				<p>
					RSA-SHA256 is the default signing algorithm. Choose <code>SHA256_WITH_ED25519</code> with a matching Ed25519 key
					for Ed25519-SHA256; both choices cover the final outgoing representation, including selected S/MIME or OpenPGP protection.
					SJM defaults to relaxed canonicalization for both headers and body. The normal configuration leaves the DKIM body-length
					tag off and signs the default header list. Turn on the
					<code class="inline language-java">l=</code> body-length tag only when a known downstream service appends a footer:
					content appended after the signed length is not covered by the signature.
				</p>
				<p>
					Likewise, exclude a header only when that specific relay rewrites it. <code class="inline language-java">From</code>
					is mandatory in a DKIM signature, so the DKIM module rejects that exclusion when signing, before reading message content.
				</p>
				<p>
					Inspect a builder's choices before building it, or clear just an optional preference when reusing it. For example,
					<code>clearSigningAlgorithm()</code>, <code>clearHeaderCanonicalization()</code> and
					<code>clearExcludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList()</code> leave the key and domain untouched.
					S/MIME offers <code>clearSignatureAlgorithm()</code>, <code>clearKeyEncapsulationAlgorithm()</code> and
					<code>clearCipherAlgorithm()</code>. Clearing restores the downstream library's choice rather than selecting another algorithm.
					Keep these objects and their serialized forms private even though automatic rendering hides known key material.
				</p>
				<p>Completed DKIM header exclusions are immutable, including after restoring a supported older serialized configuration.
					A collection returned by the getter cannot change an Email's signing choices. Build a replacement <code>DkimConfig</code>
					when selecting different exclusions; older stored entries keep their meaning.</p>
				<p>In the unreleased 10.0.0 line, <code>dkimSigningDomain(null)</code> and <code>dkimSelector(null)</code> reject the
					missing argument before replacing an earlier choice. Handle a missing domain or selector before calling the setter;
					leave it uncalled when you intend to keep the current choice. Builder inspection stays passive, and optional algorithm
					nulls still select the module default. See the <a href="migration-notes-10.0.0.html#grouped-configuration">signing configuration migration guidance</a>.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="small">DkimConfig.builder()
    .dkimPrivateKeyPath(Path.of("secrets/dkim-private-key.der"))
    .dkimSigningDomain("your-domain.org")
    .dkimSelector("dkim1")
    // Only if a known service appends a footer:
    .useLengthParam(true)
    // Only for headers that this relay rewrites:
    .excludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList("Message-ID", "Date")
    .build();
</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<p class="wide">
				You can also sign a MIME message directly. Keep the returned message and send or write that value;
				the input message does not receive the DKIM signature. In unreleased 10.0.0, the helper finalizes and
				signs the content during this call, and repeated writes retain the signed bytes:
			</p>
			<div class="wide">
				<pre><code class="small">MimeMessage signed = MailerHelper.signMessageWithDKIM(
    mimeMessageToSign, emailContainingSigningDetails);
signed.writeTo(outputStream); // Or submit signed through your application-owned transport.</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p><strong>Excluding headers</strong></p>
				<p>
					Header exclusions are a compatibility setting, not a normal tuning option. Leave the default list alone unless a
					specific relay is known to rewrite a header. <code class="inline language-java">From</code> must remain signed and
					cannot be excluded.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="small">DkimConfig.builder()
    // ... your signing key, domain and selector
    .excludedHeadersFromDkimDefaultSigningList("Message-ID", "Date")
    .build()
</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-sending-smime" id="section-sending-smime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 smime=true}}Signing / encrypting emails with S/MIME{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
    {{> components/pattern-examples topic="protected-content"}}

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Simple Java Mail supports signing and encrypting with <a href="https://www.globalsign.com/en/blog/what-is-s-mime/">S/MIME</a>.
				</p>
				<br/>
				<p>
					You can sign, encrypt or both sign and encrypt an email. In the latter case the email will first be signed
					and then encrypted, as per
					<a href="https://github.com/markenwerk/java-utils-mail-smime#using-the-library-as-a-sender-" target="_blank">
						advice</a> of the underlying library. All signing/encrypting is performed when the email is being sent.
				</p>
				<br/>
				<p>
					You can <strong>sign individual emails</strong> or <strong>sign all emails</strong> by configuring S/MIME defaults on the
					<em>Mailer</em> or through properties.
				</p>
				<p><code>clearSmimeSigning()</code> and <code>clearSmimeEncryption()</code> remove one local choice independently;
					<code>clearSmime()</code> removes both. These operations retain recipient certificates and permit ordinary template fallback.
					Use the per-field suppression controls when a template should not supply the cleared choice again. Recipient certificates can
					still request encryption after the Email choices are cleared. The <a href="understanding-configuration.html#configuration-precedence">scope and precedence table</a>
					explains these separate sources; ordinary suppression cannot remove configuration locks.</p>
				<p><code>signWithSmime(config)</code> and <code>encryptWithSmime(config)</code> require a non-null configuration.
                    A missing value throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> before changing an earlier choice; use the named clear methods
                    when removal is intentional. Optional algorithm fields may still use null to select their documented module defaults.</p>
                <p>Inside the configuration builders, <code>pkcs12Config(Pkcs12Config)</code> and
                    <code>x509Certificate(X509Certificate)</code> also require non-null material. A missing key-store configuration or
                    certificate throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> immediately, retaining the earlier material and all algorithm
                    preferences. Supply a replacement only when it is available. Selecting these values performs no cryptography and
                    does not load the S/MIME module; certificates retain their supplied identity.</p>
                <p>In unreleased 10.0.0, <code>Pkcs12Config.build()</code> requires store bytes, a store password, a key alias and a key password.
                    Missing choices fail with <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> identifying the field. Supply <code>""</code> or an empty
                    <code>char[]</code> for an explicitly empty password. The builder getters can still inspect incomplete choices;
                    null setter arguments leave earlier choices intact. Store parsing, password correctness and alias existence are checked
                    by the S/MIME operation when it uses the configuration.</p>
				<p>
					For encryption, Simple Java Mail can use one email-level encryption certificate, or different certificates per recipient. When any
					To/Cc/Bcc recipient carries a certificate, it requests S/MIME encryption even without an email-level encryption configuration.
					That recipient certificate is used first. Recipients without a certificate fall back to the
					email-level or mailer-level <code class="inline language-java">SmimeEncryptionConfig</code>, if one is configured.
					An email using these recipient certificates cannot also request <a href="#section-sending-openpgp">OpenPGP signing or encryption</a>;
					<code>buildEmail()</code> rejects that conflict.
				</p>
				<p>
					For a mailing list, department, or any group that should share certificate rules, use <code class="inline language-java">mail.recipientsBuilder()</code> before adding the
					flat recipient list to the email. <code class="inline language-java">withDefaultSmimeCertificate(...)</code> fills only missing certificates,
					<code class="inline language-java">withFixedSmimeCertificate(...)</code> replaces every recipient certificate in that group, and
					<code class="inline language-java">clearingSmimeCertificates()</code> removes certificate state from reused recipients so the email-level
					or mailer-level fallback certificate can apply.
					To keep each original recipient's certificate instead, use <code class="inline language-java">resetSmimeCertificatePolicy()</code>.
					It removes the group rule, not the recipients' certificates, and leaves any group notification policy unchanged.
				</p>
				<p><code>withSmimeCertificate(...)</code>, <code>withDefaultSmimeCertificate(...)</code> and <code>withFixedSmimeCertificate(...)</code>
                    require a non-null certificate. Passing null throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> before changing the existing certificate or group policy.
                    Use <code>clearingSmimeCertificate()</code> for an individual recipient, or the group clear/reset operations above when removal or restoration is intended.</p>
				<table><thead><tr><th>Recipient-group operation</th><th>Certificates on produced recipients</th></tr></thead><tbody>
					<tr><td><code>withDefaultSmimeCertificate(c)</code></td><td>Fill missing certificates only.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>withFixedSmimeCertificate(c)</code></td><td>Replace every recipient certificate with <code>c</code>.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>clearingSmimeCertificates()</code></td><td>Strip certificates, permitting the Email fallback.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>resetSmimeCertificatePolicy()</code></td><td>Stop applying the group rule and preserve the original recipients' certificates.</td></tr>
				</tbody></table>
				<br/>
				<p>
					For maximum flexibility, you can configure all algorithms and certificates specific to S/MIME signing and encryption. This includes
					choosing the key encapsulation algorithm and cipher algorithm for encryption, and the signature algorithm for signing. For a list of
					available algorithms, see the <code class="inline language-java">SmimeEncryptionConfig</code> and
					<code class="inline language-java">SmimeSigningConfig</code> classes.
				</p>
				<p>In the unreleased 10.0.0 line, S/MIME retains the finalized content headers and the
                    <a href="features.html#smime-body-transfer-encoding">body transfer encoding you selected</a> for single-part and multipart
                    messages alike. Signing and encryption wrap that prepared content without choosing a different inner-body encoding.
                    Before signing, raw binary attachments receive a safe transfer encoding so MIME line canonicalization cannot change their decoded bytes.
                    This preparation leaves the caller's content unchanged and keeps binary attachments supported.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
				Signing an email:
				<pre><code>Pkcs12Config myKeyInfo = Pkcs12Config.builder()
    .pkcs12Store(Path.of("my_smime_keystore.pkcs12"))
    .storePassword("my_store_password")
    .keyAlias("my_key_alias")
    .keyPassword("my_key_password")
    .build();

Email emailToBeSigned = currentEmailBuilder
    .(..)
    .signWithSmime(SmimeSigningConfig.builder()
		.pkcs12Config(myKeyInfo)
		.signatureAlgorithm("SHA256withRSA") // optional
		.build())
    .buildEmail();

mailer.sync().sendMail(emailToBeSigned);
</code></pre>
				Encrypting an email:
				<pre><code>Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .(..)
    .encryptWithSmime(SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
		.x509Certificate(Path.of("x509CertificateInStandardPEM.crt"))
		.keyEncapsulationAlgorithm("RSA_OAEP_SHA384") // optional
		.cipherAlgorithm("AES256_CBC") // optional
		.build())
    .buildEmail();

mailer.sync().sendMail(emailToBeEncrypted);
</code></pre>
				Encrypting with different recipient certificates:
				<pre><code>SmimeEncryptionConfig algorithmsAndFallback = SmimeEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .x509Certificate(fallbackCertificate)
    .keyEncapsulationAlgorithm("RSA_OAEP_SHA384")
    .cipherAlgorithm("AES256_CBC")
    .build();

Recipient alice = mail.recipientBuilder()
    .withAddress("alice@example.com")
    .withType(Message.RecipientType.TO)
    .withSmimeCertificate(aliceCertificate)
    .build();

Recipient bob = mail.recipientBuilder()
    .withAddress("bob@example.com")
    .withType(Message.RecipientType.TO)
    .withSmimeCertificate(bobCertificate)
    .build();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(alice, bob)
    .encryptWithSmime(algorithmsAndFallback)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
				Encrypting a group with exactly one certificate:
				<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; finance = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .withFixedSmimeCertificate(financeCertificate)
    .withRecipientsWithDefaultName("Finance", Message.RecipientType.TO,
            "alice@example.com", "bob@example.com")
    .buildRecipients();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(finance)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
				Defaulting only missing certificates in a group:
				<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; finance = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .withDefaultSmimeCertificate(financeFallbackCertificate)
    .withRecipients(previousFinanceRecipients)
    .buildRecipients();
// recipients that already had their own certificates keep them;
// the others receive financeFallbackCertificate</code></pre>
				Clearing reused recipient certificates so the email-level certificate applies:
				<pre><code>List&lt;Recipient&gt; reusableRecipients = SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().recipientsBuilder()
    .clearingSmimeCertificates()
    .withRecipients(previousRecipients)
    .buildRecipients();

Email emailToBeEncrypted = currentEmailBuilder
    .withRecipients(reusableRecipients)
    .encryptWithSmime(emailLevelSmimeEncryptConfig)
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
				Resetting a group rule preserves the original metadata instead:
				<pre><code class="language-java">IRecipientsBuilder group = mail.recipientsBuilder()
    .withRecipients(previousRecipients);

List&lt;Recipient&gt; cleared =
    group.clearingSmimeCertificates().buildRecipients();

List&lt;Recipient&gt; preserved =
    group.resetSmimeCertificatePolicy().buildRecipients();</code></pre>
				<p>The previously built <code>cleared</code> list remains cleared, and the source recipients are unchanged.
					Resetting this certificate policy leaves the group's NOTIFY policy unchanged. Neither the group clear nor the Email's
					<code>clearSmime()</code> is a universal encryption-disable operation: Email templates or recipients added directly to the Email later can still supply
					protection choices.</p>
				Sign all emails by default from Java:
				<pre><code>SmimeSigningConfig signingDefaults = SmimeSigningConfig.builder()
    .pkcs12Config(myKeyInfo)
    .build();

SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().mailerBuilder()
    (...)
    .withEmailDefaults(SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults().emailBuilder().startingBlank()
		.signWithSmime(signingDefaults)
		.buildEmailCompletedWithDefaultsAndOverrides()) // retain property defaults too
    .buildMailer();
</code></pre>
				Property-only S/MIME defaults:
				<pre><code class="language-properties small"># Sign every email sent through a property-configured mailer.
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore=my_smime_keystore.pkcs12
simplejavamail.smime.signing.keystore_password=keystore_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_alias=key_alias
simplejavamail.smime.signing.key_password=key_password
simplejavamail.smime.signing.algorithm=SHA256withRSA

# Encrypt with one fallback certificate and the selected algorithms.
# Recipient-level certificates configured in Java still take precedence.
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.certificate=shared-recipient-certificate.pem
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.key_encapsulation_algorithm=RSA
# AES is recommended; DES_EDE3_CBC remains available for legacy recipient compatibility.
simplejavamail.smime.encryption.cipher=AES256_CBC

# Optional when the application is fully property-driven.
simplejavamail.defaults.to.name=Finance
simplejavamail.defaults.to.address=alice@example.com,bob@example.com
</code></pre>
				<p class="small">
					Properties can configure the signing identity and a single encryption fallback. They cannot express different S/MIME certificates per
					recipient; use <code class="inline language-java">mail.recipientBuilder()</code> or <code class="inline language-java">mail.recipientsBuilder()</code>
					for per-recipient or group certificate policy.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-smime-key-cache" id="section-smime-key-cache" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		<h3>Choose S/MIME key reuse capacity</h3>
		<section><div class="wide full-width-example">
			<p>When an application signs or decrypts with many different key configurations, choose how many loaded configurations it may keep
				for reuse. Configure <code>simplejavamail.smime.cache.capacity</code> on the <code>SimpleJavaMail</code> factory:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-java">SimpleJavaMail mail = SimpleJavaMail.withConfig(
    ConfigLoader.builder()
        .withMap(Map.of(ConfigKeys.SMIME_KEY_CACHE_CAPACITY.key(), 10_000))
        .load());</code></pre>
			<p>The example chooses 10,000 entries. The published fallback,
				<code>ConfigLoader.DEFAULT_SMIME_KEY_CACHE_CAPACITY</code>, is <code>-1</code>:</p>
			<div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable S/MIME key reuse capacity values">
				<table><thead><tr><th>Configured value</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
					<tr><td>Absent or <code>-1</code></td><td>Keep entries without a count limit while the application retains the factory or its participating Mailers and converters.</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Keep no completed entries. Signing and decryption still load and use keys; overlapping work can share a load.</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Positive integer</td><td>Keep at most that many completed configurations. A new entry removes the least recently used entry when space is needed.</td></tr>
				</tbody></table>
			</div>
			<p>This counts key configurations, including their retained keystore/password material and parsed keys. It is not a byte or heap limit.
				An operation already using a key can finish after eviction; a later operation may reload it from the material held by the configuration. Entries do not expire
				with time. Values below <code>-1</code> are rejected before key loading for signing or decryption; fractions, overflow and unsupported
				input types are configuration errors.</p>
			<p>Mailers and converters obtained from the same factory share this capacity and reuse. For example, incoming decryption uses the
				same factory as sending:</p>
			<pre><code class="language-java">Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(incomingEml)
    .withSmimeDecryption(customerKeys)
    .toEmail();</code></pre>
			<p>Separate factories have independent caches, even if they use the same configuration snapshot. A send uses the selected Mailer's
				factory, including a borrowed failover Mailer from another factory; the factory that originally built the Email does not choose it.
				Private named Mailers share their creating factory's capacity. Put the property at the configuration root; a named-Mailer definition
				cannot override it. The fixed-value form is <code>simplejavamail.locked.smime.cache.capacity</code>.</p>
			<p>To change capacity, create a replacement configured factory and obtain its Mailers and converters. Existing objects keep their
				selected policy. Close Mailers as usual to release sending resources; closing one does not clear keys shared with other participants.
				Retaining a factory, one of its converters or a Mailer can retain its cached keys. No additional cache cleanup call is required.
				<code>SimpleJavaMail.fromDefaults()</code> and static conversion helpers use the conventional default factory.</p>
		</div></section>


        <a href="#section-reading-smime" id="section-reading-smime" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 smime=true}}Reading S/MIME messages and attachments{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

        <section><div class="view">
            <p>Convert supplied EML or Outlook MSG with the incoming keys your application makes available. Successfully opened content is readable
                through the ordinary Email getters: bodies stay bodies, files stay attachments, and an independent attached message stays one attachment.
                Reading a getter never starts decryption, verification or another input read.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">Email received = mail.converter()
    .fromEml(Path.of("partner-message.eml"))
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();

String body = received.getPlainText();
AttachmentResource document = received.getAttachments().get(0);
byte[] usableDocument = document.readAllBytes();
Email attachedMessage = document.getEmail(); // Already parsed, or null.
</code></pre>
            <p>An independently protected PDF exposes the recovered PDF through that same resource when opening succeeds. Its complete protected input
                remains available separately through <code>received.getInspection().getOriginalAttachment(0)</code>. A file inside a whole-message
                encrypted envelope can have the same PDF as its original attachment; the enclosing envelope belongs to
                <code>getOriginalMessage()</code>. Names are not unique identifiers, and an attachment's position identifies the occurrence being inspected.</p>
            <p>When one valid configured key cannot open a particular file, readable neighbors remain available and the file keeps its protected payload
                with a source-linked Problem. Corrupt or unsupported input also keeps available evidence. An unusable configured keystore, an explicitly
                requested missing module, cancellation and unrelated application failures still fail the operation. Invalid signatures do not discard
                readable content. The <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection">received-protection workflow</a> shows how an application can decide whether to act on it.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-verification" id="section-reading-smime-verification" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}Read the protection of the value you use{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>A valid signature over plain text does not verify native HTML, a Subject or an independently attached child's files. Inspect the exact
                selected value with <code>getPlainText()</code>, <code>getHTMLText()</code>, <code>getSubject()</code> or <code>getAttachment(index)</code>
                on <code>EmailInspection</code>. These return metadata, while the useful content remains on Email and AttachmentResource.</p>
            <p><code>VERIFIED</code> means established full signature coverage of that value; it does not approve the signer. <code>INVALID</code>
                means an actual signature check failed. <code>UNVERIFIED</code> is a recognized associated signature whose verification was not
                established. <code>UNSIGNED</code> needs established absence. <code>UNKNOWN</code> means presence or coverage could not be established.
                <code>MIXED</code> preserves differing outcomes or known partial coverage. Inspect individual observations when a summary needs explanation.</p>
            <p>The family view, <code>received.getInspection().getSmime()</code>, lists captured S/MIME signature and decryption observations, algorithms,
                available signer-key facts and source references. It includes the actual opening layers of this message's direct resources as source-scoped
                history, including after a resource is cleared or replaced. It does not declare the parent body or whole Email trusted. An attached child's
                internal body observations remain on <code>attachment.getEmail().getInspection()</code> when that child is available.</p>
            <p>A supplied certificate approves a signing key only when your application chooses it. SJM does not discover a certificate path, check
                revocation or make a claimed From address trustworthy. <code>ApprovedSigners</code> below pins explicitly configured signing public keys;
                certificate identity, validity and organizational authorization remain your application's responsibility.</p>
            <p id="nested-smime-ciphertext">Nested protection retains individual layer observations. A successful outer decryption does not establish
                the signature or encryption coverage of an unopened inner value. Missing or malformed MIME declarations retain unknown facts and
                their actual Problems; they do not establish plain, unsigned content. Explicit keys and normal shared import limits apply during conversion.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-merging" id="section-reading-smime-merging" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}One readable message, with retained originals{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>Conversion selects this logical message's representations rather than merging an outer Email with a second inner Email. Protected body
                content supplies an available plain or HTML alternative, including a present empty string. When that alternative is absent or unavailable,
                an already materialized native alternative can fill it. Each alternative has its own source, derivation and protection evidence.</p>
            <p>HTML keeps its associated embedded resources. An unambiguous already read same-message resource can fill a missing reference; an independent
                child's images are never borrowed. Materialized images unused by the selected HTML remain ordinary attachments. Ambiguity keeps its
                source-linked Problem rather than creating a verified resource association. Calendar method and text are selected together.</p>
            <p>A present readable protected Subject takes precedence, including an explicitly empty string; otherwise an available earlier/native Subject
                can supply the selected fallback. A missing or unavailable final Subject is null. Recognized protected authored headers supply the logical
                addresses, identity, stated sent date and threading with their meaningful omissions. Body-only protection leaves those native headers in place.
                Generated adapter headers and independently attached messages do not supply the parent's
                identity. The normal case-insensitive <code>getHeaders()</code> read view keeps useful represented received and custom values; it is not an exact wire dump.</p>
            <p>When input positively identifies a protected authored address view but that view cannot be read, automatic replies leave destinations
                unresolved for the caller to choose. Available outer metadata remains readable with its actual source and protection facts.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">AttachmentResource originalMessage = received.getInspection().getOriginalMessage();
AttachmentResource originalDocument = received.getInspection().getOriginalAttachment(0);

Email edited = mail.emailBuilder().copying(received)
    .withPlainText("Replacement body")
    .buildEmail();
// The new plain text is caller-supplied with unknown incoming protection.
// Unchanged fields keep their evidence; retained originals remain historical input.
</code></pre>
            <p>Complete raw EML and MSG supplied to conversion are retained automatically under its import contract, in their actual format. A supplied
                MimeMessage without captured raw input has no invented original file. Incomplete prefixes remain diagnostic source evidence and cannot
                be forwarded as complete originals. Captured storage can be shared by ordinary copies and snapshots; raw originals and genuinely different
                decoded content can still require additional memory. There is no whole-JVM memory promise or automatic temporary-file spill.</p>
            <p>For EML String input, retained bytes are the UTF-8 representation the converter actually consumed. They do not establish an unknown external wire encoding.</p>
            <p>Current field and indexed resource inspections describe the selected values after edits. Family observations retain their historical
                source scope; use the current value's inspection for a requirements check, rather than treating an old family observation as protection of a replacement.</p>
            <p>Reading a child Email leaves its parent's selected attachment payload unchanged. Edit a child, render the replacement and replace the parent
                attachment explicitly when that is your intent. Source data and Problems are read separately through
                <a href="features.html#section-source-inspection">source inspection</a>; they are never extra outgoing MIME attachments.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-smime-decryption" id="section-reading-smime-decryption" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 smime=true}}Supply incoming S/MIME keys{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <pre><code class="language-java">Pkcs12Config recipientKeys = Pkcs12Config.builder()
    .pkcs12Store(Path.of("recipient.p12"))
    .storePassword(storePassword)
    .keyAlias("recipient")
    .keyPassword(keyPassword)
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter()
    .fromOutlookMsg(Path.of("partner-message.msg"))
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();
</code></pre>
            <p>Use <code>fromEml(...)</code> for EML input. Requesting decryption requires <code>smime-module</code>; ordinary recognition/import without that
                capability remains available. Key reuse belongs to the converter's configured factory, under its
                <a href="#section-smime-key-cache">S/MIME key reuse capacity</a>. A getter never loads keys or retries a failed attempt.</p>
            <p id="signed-parent-attachments">The supplied keys also apply to supported independent protected attachments in signed or OpenPGP content.
                Successful opening exposes their usable ordinary resources. Their observations remain associated with those resources; an encrypted
                attachment does not make every parent field decrypted.</p>
            <p>For a selected value, <code>DECRYPTED</code> means full established recovery from encrypted input; <code>ENCRYPTED</code> means its selected
                payload is recognized unopened ciphertext; <code>NOT_ENCRYPTED</code> needs established absence; <code>UNKNOWN</code> keeps unresolved
                coverage. Detailed attempts distinguish <code>DEFERRED</code>, <code>UNAVAILABLE</code> and <code>FAILED</code>. Decryption alone does not approve a sender.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection" id="section-inspecting-received-protection" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        <h2>Check incoming mail before acting on it</h2>
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>A partner sends an encrypted, signed document for automatic processing. First convert it with the receive keys you have, then select
                the exact attachment your application requires. Parsing successfully, seeing readable text or finding an empty Problems list does not
                establish that the document meets your protection policy.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">ApprovedSigners approvedPartner = ApprovedSigners.builder()
    .addSmimeCertificate(currentPartnerCertificate)
    .addSmimeCertificate(replacementPartnerCertificate)
    .build();

ProtectionRequirements documentRequirements = ProtectionRequirements.builder()
    .requireVerifiedSignatureFrom(approvedPartner)
    .requireDecryptedContent()
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(input)
    .withSmimeDecryption(recipientKeys)
    .toEmail();

int documentIndex = findRequiredDocument(received.getAttachments()); // Application choice.
AttachmentResource document = received.getAttachments().get(documentIndex);
ProtectionCheck check = documentRequirements.check(
    received.getInspection().getAttachment(documentIndex));

if (check.isSatisfied()) {
    processDocument(document.readAllBytes());
} else {
    holdForReview(check.getOutcome(), check.getReasons(), received.getProblems());
}
</code></pre>
            <p>The document lookup and process/hold functions belong to the application. Choose by your expected document contract, and retain the
                attachment occurrence; duplicate names need not identify the same input. A parsed attached Email has its own inspection. Checking the
                parent's whole attachment does not silently substitute a check of one child body.</p>
            <p><code>requireVerifiedSignatureFrom(...)</code> requires one same signature that is verified, fully covers the selected value and uses an
                explicitly approved actual signing key. A valid unapproved signer cannot combine with an invalid approved signer. Renewed S/MIME
                certificates with the same normalized public key can match; a new key needs explicit approval. This is signing-key pinning, not automatic PKI validation.</p>
            <p>For OpenPGP, configure <code>ApprovedSigners.builder().addOpenPgpSigningKeyFingerprint(fullFingerprint).build()</code> with the full
                fingerprint of the actual signing key, including the signing subkey when applicable. Short key IDs do not approve a key, and approving
                one key does not automatically approve unrelated or unvalidated subkeys. Receive-key configuration enables verification; it is separate from approval.</p>
            <p><code>requireDecryptedContent()</code> checks that this selected content was recovered from encryption. A plain PDF and an unopened encrypted
                resource fail that requirement. It does not attempt decryption. The requirements value can be reused for HTML, Subject or one resource
                inspection at a time; checking a document says nothing about unselected fields.</p>
            <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Protection check outcomes">
                <table><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Application meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>SATISFIED</code></td><td>The supplied requirements are established for this selected value.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>NOT_SATISFIED</code></td><td>At least one requirement is positively unmet, such as an unsigned document or a definitely unapproved signing key.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>INDETERMINATE</code></td><td>Unavailable evidence prevents a decision, such as an unverified candidate that might otherwise qualify.</td></tr>
                </tbody></table>
            </div>
            <p>Reasons have stable codes, explanations and captured source references. They belong to the returned check and do not add Email Problems.
                A recognized unsigned message can have no processing Problems while failing a required-signature rule. A missing value differs from a
                present empty value. Deferred, unavailable and uncaptured history do not become successful empty checks.</p>
            <h3 id="received-protection-explanations">Explain the selected value</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-java">ValueInspection html = received.getInspection().getHTMLText();
html.getOrigin();
html.getDerivation();
html.getAvailability();
html.getSignatureStatus();
html.getEncryptionStatus();

for (SignatureInspection signature : html.getSignatures()) {
    ProtectionCoverage coverage = signature.getCoverage();
    SignatureObservation observation = signature.getObservation();
    SignatureVerification verification = observation.getStatus();
    String signingKey = observation.getSigningKeyFingerprint();
    // Explain this observation and its captured sources without verifying again.
}
</code></pre>
            <p>A wholly signed and encrypted HTML representation can have <code>VERIFIED</code>, <code>DECRYPTED</code> and full coverage. If protected
                content supplies only plain text while the native message supplies HTML, both remain readable; checking the plain text cannot approve
                that fallback HTML. Its own inspection describes its native/derived origin and actual coverage. Native origin alone is not proof of
                unsigned content: an enclosing layer may itself cover it.</p>
            <p>One full verified approved signature plus another invalid signature yields a <code>MIXED</code> summary and can satisfy the base
                approved-signature requirement. Add <code>requireAllSignaturesVerified()</code> deliberately when every relevant associated signature
                must verify. That stricter choice still needs the approved-signature requirement; it does not approve all signers. Known partial coverage
                remains <code>PARTIAL</code> and cannot be the full-coverage witness. Unknown coverage remains uncertain. Requirements evaluate one selected value per check.</p>
            <p>Keep the complete original input when your application needs evidence or later original-preserving forwarding. Use
                <code>received.getInspection().getOriginalMessage()</code> and <code>getOriginalAttachment(documentIndex)</code>; null means no suitable
                complete captured input was retained. Do not rebuild a signed archive from getters and call it the original. Protect your stored plaintext,
                original input and credentials under your own retention/access policy.</p>
            <h3 id="received-protection-reuse">Reply or forward deliberately</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-java">Email interpretedForward = mail.emailBuilder().forwarding(received)
    .from("operator@example.org").to("reviewer@example.org")
    .encryptWithSmime(reviewerEncryption)
    .buildEmail();

Email originalForward = mail.emailBuilder().forwardingOriginal(received)
    .from("operator@example.org").to("reviewer@example.org")
    .buildEmail();
</code></pre>
            <p>The interpreted forward uses represented content, including edits and recovered document bytes. The original forward carries retained input
                in its actual EML or MSG format and fails clearly if suitable complete input is unavailable. It does not rewrite the original payload.
                Reading a parsed child does not change either attachment payload. An intact encrypted original can be forwarded in an unencrypted outer
                message when no other derived value discloses confidential content; it does not grant the new recipient a decryption key.</p>
            <p>A child's retained original contains that child's EML or MSG, without its parent's encryption envelope. If it was extracted through the
                parent's encryption, forwarding that original still reuses confidential content and needs outgoing encryption or the conversion's explicit
                permission. Even an encrypted child body can leave its own headers clear inside that original file. A signature alone does not establish confidentiality.</p>
            <p>For known confidential input, the new original-forward carrier uses a neutral format-based filename and omits inherited description/CID
                metadata. The original resource getter keeps its actual input metadata and bytes.</p>
            <p>Reply and interpreted-forward helpers require suitable outgoing encryption when they automatically reuse confidential content. Drafts remain
                readable and editable. Enforcement happens at final MIME/EML output and sending after outgoing choices have resolved, including suitable
                enclosing encryption. Incoming credentials are not outgoing protection settings.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">Email permittedReply = mail.emailBuilder()
    .replyingTo(received, ReplyOptions.allowUnencryptedContent())
    .from("operator@example.org")
    .buildEmail();

byte[] handoff = mail.converter().fromEmail(confidentialDraft)
    .allowUnencryptedContent()
    .toEmlBytes();
// This conversion's permission leaves confidentialDraft unchanged.
// A fresh conversion or later send still needs protection or draft permission.
</code></pre>
            <p><code>ForwardOptions.allowUnencryptedContent()</code> supplies the corresponding deliberate forward permission. Neither permission disables
                configured or mandatory outgoing encryption or allows fallback when encryption fails. Ordinary copies/snapshots preserve a draft's own
                choice; a new reply or forward uses its own options. Editing one body does not waive a remaining quote. Explicitly clearing every reused
                confidential body/resource removes those components' requirement; merely replacing text does not prove the quote is gone.</p>
            <p>The received getter keeps a private Subject. A new helper-generated public Subject uses an actually known public counterpart, otherwise
                <code>Re: Your message</code> or <code>Fwd: Message</code>. Permission to reuse body content leaves that safe default in place. Body
                encryption does not hide every outer header; this is not full outbound header protection. See
                <a href="features.html#section-reply-forward">reply/forward details</a>, the
                <a href="email-workload-field-guide.html#check-a-partner-document-before-processing-it">partner-document recipe</a> and the
                <a href="patterns/protected-partners.html">runnable protected-partners application</a>.</p>
        </div></section>

		<a href="#section-sending-openpgp" id="section-sending-openpgp" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h2 openpgp=true}}Signing / encrypting emails with OpenPGP/MIME{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					The optional <code class="inline language-text">openpgp-module</code> adds
					<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3156.html">RFC 3156</a> signing and encryption. Give it armored or binary key-ring bytes from
					your application's key store. The module does not look up keys on WKD or a public keyserver.
				</p>
				<p>
					You can sign, encrypt, or do both. When both are configured, Simple Java Mail signs the clear MIME entity first and then encrypts it.
					Add one public key ring for every key that should be able to decrypt the message. This list is separate from the email's To, Cc, and Bcc
					recipients, so your application remains responsible for choosing the right keys.
				</p>
				<p>
					S/MIME and OpenPGP/MIME are alternative content-protection formats, so one email cannot configure both. DKIM can be combined with
					either format and is applied after content protection to the final outgoing message.
				</p>
				<p>
					A certificate on a To/Cc/Bcc recipient is an S/MIME encryption request, including certificates supplied by recipient-group defaults or
					fixed policies. Combining it with OpenPGP signing or encryption throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> during
					<code>buildEmail()</code>. The error identifies the conflicting recipient or email-level choices. This check uses metadata;
					it does not load protection modules, inspect key material or read message content.
				</p>
				<p>
					Completion checks again after applying defaults, overrides and mandatory locks. A conflict supplied by the selected Mailer's policy
					therefore fails before MIME protection or submission. Remove the unwanted choice explicitly. <code>clearSmime()</code> removes only
					email-level choices; to reuse certified recipients for OpenPGP, strip certificates from copies and replace the copied email's recipients.
					The original email and recipients keep their settings. Template suppression can exclude ordinary template choices; locks still apply.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
				Add the module:
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;openpgp-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>

				Sign and encrypt:
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpSigningConfig signing = OpenPgpSigningConfig.builder()
    .secretKeyRing(senderSecretKeyRing)
    .passphrase(senderPassphrase)
    .build();

OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(alicePublicKeyRing)
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(bobPublicKeyRing)
    .build();

Email protectedEmail = currentEmailBuilder
    .signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .encryptWithOpenPgp(encryption)
    .buildEmail();

mailer.sync().sendMail(protectedEmail);</code></pre>

				Reuse certified recipients for OpenPGP:
<pre><code class="language-java">List&lt;Recipient&gt; pgpRecipients = mail.recipientsBuilder()
    .withRecipients(smimeEmail.getRecipients())
    .clearingSmimeCertificates()
    .buildRecipients();

Email pgpEmail = mail.emailBuilder().copying(smimeEmail)
    .clearSmime()
    .clearRecipients()
    .withRecipients(pgpRecipients)
    .signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .buildEmail();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>To reuse OpenPGP settings, put them on an Email template and pass it to <code>withEmailDefaults(...)</code> or
					<code>withEmailOverrides(...)</code>, just as you would for S/MIME or DKIM. Each signing or encryption configuration is one complete
					choice: an override replaces the supplied choice, which otherwise wins over a default. Encryption key-ring lists are not merged.</p>
				<p><code>clearOpenPgpSigning()</code> and <code>clearOpenPgpEncryption()</code> remove one local choice independently;
					<code>clearOpenPgp()</code> removes both. They permit template fallback. To exclude a template instead, use the ordinary
					defaults/overrides suppression controls with <code>OPENPGP_SIGNING_CONFIG</code> or <code>OPENPGP_ENCRYPTION_CONFIG</code>.
					Exact EML remains untouched, including after Java serialization.</p>
				<p>On the signing-config builder, <code>clearSigningKeyId()</code> returns to automatic key selection and
					<code>resetHashAlgorithm()</code> restores SHA-256. The encryption-config builder's <code>resetSymmetricAlgorithm()</code>
					restores AES-256. These companions keep the supplied keys and passphrases; previously built configurations do not change.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">Email protectionDefaults = mail.emailBuilder().startingBlank()
    .signWithOpenPgp(signing)
    .encryptWithOpenPgp(encryption)
    .buildEmail();

Mailer protectedMailer = mail.mailerBuilder()
    .withEmailDefaults(protectionDefaults)
    .buildMailer();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p><strong>Keys and algorithm choices</strong></p>
				<p>
					Signing uses SHA-256 by default; SHA-384 and SHA-512 are also available. Encryption uses AES-256 by default; AES-128 and AES-192 are
					available for interoperability needs. You can also select a signing key ID when a secret key ring contains more than one signing key.
				</p>
				<p>
					Key-ring data and passphrases are copied into the configuration object and hidden from <code class="inline language-java">toString()</code>.
					Both configurations are serializable and are retained when an <code class="inline language-java">Email</code> is serialized, including the
					selected signing key, hash and cipher. A signing snapshot contains the secret key ring and passphrase; encryption-only configuration
					contains recipient public key rings and the cipher preference. Protect these snapshots as you would S/MIME private keys and passwords.
					See <a href="features.html#section-serializing-emails">Serializing Email objects</a> for a round-trip example and older-stream limitations.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpSigningConfig signing = OpenPgpSigningConfig.builder()
    .secretKeyRing(senderSecretKeyRing)
    .passphrase(senderPassphrase)
    .signingKeyId(signingKeyId) // optional
    .hashAlgorithm(OpenPgpSigningConfig.HashAlgorithm.SHA512)
    .build();

OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(recipientPublicKeyRing)
    .symmetricAlgorithm(
        OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.SymmetricAlgorithm.AES_256)
    .build();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


		<a href="#section-openpgp-key-replacement" id="section-openpgp-key-replacement" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
		{{#> components/feature-heading-h3 openpgp=true}}Replacing recipient keys{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}

		<section>
			<div class="view">
				<p>
					Use <code>addRecipientPublicKeyRing(...)</code> to append a recipient key, or <code>recipientPublicKeyRings(...)</code> to replace
					the complete selection. Replacement keeps the selected cipher and copies both the list and its byte arrays. A null list or a null
					entry is rejected before changing your earlier choices. An empty list deliberately clears the keys, just like
					<code>clearRecipientPublicKeyRings()</code>.
				</p>
				<p>
					Setters collect your choices; <code>getRecipientPublicKeyRings()</code> returns an editable copy without validating unfinished
					key material. Both ways of supplying keys accept non-null empty byte arrays while you configure the builder, but <code>build()</code>
					still requires at least one key ring and rejects every empty ring. Correct the choices and build again when needed. Actual key parsing
					remains part of applying OpenPGP protection. Editing or clearing the builder leaves previously built configurations unchanged.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="side">
<pre><code class="language-java">var recipientKeys = OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.builder()
    .addRecipientPublicKeyRing(alicePublicKeyRing)
    .symmetricAlgorithm(
        OpenPgpEncryptionConfig.SymmetricAlgorithm.AES_128);

recipientKeys.recipientPublicKeyRings(
    java.util.Arrays.asList(alicePublicKeyRing, bobPublicKeyRing));

// Inspect detached choices before building.
java.util.List&lt;byte[]&gt; selectedKeys =
    recipientKeys.getRecipientPublicKeyRings();
OpenPgpEncryptionConfig encryption = recipientKeys.build();</code></pre>
			</div>
		</section>


        <a href="#section-reading-openpgp" id="section-reading-openpgp" class="section-link h2">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h2 openpgp=true}}Reading OpenPGP/MIME messages{{/components/feature-heading-h2}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p>Supply verification public keys and available decryption secret keys while converting EML or a supplied MimeMessage. “Reading” here
                means interpreting input supplied by your application; SJM does not fetch a mailbox over IMAP or POP3. Receive keys enable processing;
                they do not automatically approve a sender for an application action.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">OpenPgpReceiveConfig receive = OpenPgpReceiveConfig.builder()
    .addVerificationKeyRing(senderPublicKeyRing)
    .addDecryptionKeyRing(recipientSecretKeyRing, recipientPassphrase)
    .build();

Email received = mail.converter().fromEml(emlInputStream)
    .withOpenPgpReceiveConfig(receive)
    .toEmail();

ProtectionInspection openPgp = received.getInspection().getOpenPgp();
ValueInspection document = received.getInspection().getAttachment(0);
</code></pre>
            <p>Successful decryption makes the selected bodies and attachment payloads usable through their normal getters. Independently attached
                messages stay child-owned. Supply <code>withSmimeDecryption(...)</code> too when supported S/MIME files need opening; each optional module
                owns its family processing and its own unavailable-module boundary. All selected nested processing shares the import's allowances.</p>
            <p>Base64 or quoted-printable signature/ciphertext transport encoding is decoded before processing. Verification uses the received signed MIME
                entity, not a reconstruction from selected values. Tolerated outer line endings for ordinary import do not relax exact-EML relay's canonical
                byte requirements. A signed entity with an empty MIME header area retains its default plain-text meaning.</p>
            <p>Use <code>passphraseProvider(...)</code> when passphrases are resolved by key ID. Returning null uses that key ring's configured passphrase.
                The provider runs only when processing needs it. An application/provider defect escapes with its original cause; it is not relabeled as bad
                ciphertext. Missing matching keys and actual input failures retain their separate processing observations.</p>
            <p id="receive-key-draft-validation">Receive configuration setters snapshot non-null key material and allow unfinished empty bytes on the builder.
                Getters return detached choices without invoking the passphrase provider; <code>build()</code> rejects empty keys. The optional module owns
                cryptographic interpretation. Use the <a href="#section-inspecting-received-protection">same requirements workflow</a> to approve an actual full signing-key fingerprint.</p>
        </div></section>

        <a href="#section-reading-openpgp-status" id="section-reading-openpgp-status" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
        {{#> components/feature-heading-h3 openpgp=true}}Inspect OpenPGP observations{{/components/feature-heading-h3}}
        <section><div class="view">
            <p><code>getOpenPgp()</code> exposes immutable individual signature/decryption observations and whether family inspection completed. Direct resources'
                actual opening layers remain source-scoped history; a parsed child's internal body evidence stays with that child. Repeated
                signatures from the same key remain distinct. Each signature retains its actual signing-key fingerprint, available key ID, algorithms,
                verification result, explanation and captured source references. It is not a whole-Email trusted Boolean.</p>
            <pre><code class="language-java">for (SignatureObservation signature : received.getInspection().getOpenPgp().getSignatures()) {
    SignatureVerification verification = signature.getStatus();
    String signingKeyFingerprint = signature.getSigningKeyFingerprint();
    String algorithm = signature.getSignatureAlgorithm();
    // Diagnostic facts; application approval remains separate.
}

ProtectionCheck decision = documentRequirements.check(
    received.getInspection().getAttachment(0));
</code></pre>
            <p>The family-neutral <a href="#section-reading-smime-verification">signature and encryption meanings</a> also apply here. A recognized
                signature without an available verification key is <code>UNVERIFIED</code>; unavailable presence/coverage is <code>UNKNOWN</code>.
                A supplied receive key does not establish decryption of an unencrypted packet in a misleading encrypted wrapper. Actual established
                decryption and individual signature results remain distinct when another selected layer cannot be processed.</p>
            <p>Without <code>openpgp-module</code>, ordinary import keeps available metadata and recognizable protected input, with unestablished results
                unknown or unavailable. An explicit receive configuration requires the module. Malformed MIME declarations and a protocol that disagrees
                with its wrapper do not establish plain, unsigned or decrypted content; available sources and actual Problems remain readable.</p>
            <p><a href="features.html#section-exact-eml">Exact EML construction</a> retains authoritative bytes and its independent SMTP envelope contract.
                Captured self-contained observations may be available, while missing support or keys leave facts unestablished. Exact mode has no receive-key
                option; use ordinary conversion when you need explicit keys or readable opened content. Inspection never rewrites exact bytes.</p>
            <p>Complete raw input is retained in its actual format and can be shared by copies. Different decoded content and captured originals can cost
                additional memory; import byte limits are not whole-JVM memory limits. Old snapshots with uncaptured history remain unknown rather than
                acquiring new proof during deserialization. Do not assume that arbitrary whole-Email optional-module snapshots are portable to another classpath.</p>
        </div></section>
